<PageLayout
	title="About Me"
	eyebrow="Who's behind this"
	description="Computer Engineering student at Palomar College, transferring to UC San Diego. Embedded systems, Meshtastic, self-hosting, and a lot of soldering."
>
	<p>
		I'm jdankney — a Computer Engineering student at Palomar College, currently
		working on transferring to UC San Diego. This site is my workbench
		notebook: parts lists, wiring diagrams, firmware notes, and an honest
		record of everything that went right (and everything that caught fire
		along the way).
	</p>

	<p>
		Most of what I build lives somewhere between hardware and software.
		Lately that's meant ESP32 and nRF52840 boards, custom Bluetooth and LoRa
		firmware, and rebuilding cheap consumer electronics from scratch once the
		original board inevitably dies. I run Arch Linux, self-host what I can,
		and I'm slowly getting better at not burning out MOSFETs.
	</p>

	<p>
		A few things I care about: building projects that actually get used
		instead of shelved, documenting the mistakes as thoroughly as the
		successes, and picking parts I can actually source again when something
		breaks.
	</p>

	<p>
		If you want to see what I'm currently working on, check out the
		<a href="/projects">project log</a>.
	</p>
</PageLayout>
